GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:29:- Store arms: `addr = relative_addr + LUNA88K_REGISTERS_BASE`, `switch (addr)` on exact labels; `cpunr = (addr - INT_ST_MASK0) / 4` in {0,1,2,3} — no OOB index anywhere, the defect is the NULL call. Latch then `d->interrupt_enable[cpunr] = idata; reassert_interrupts(d);` unconditionally; SOFT arm as inlined; `case RESET_CPU_ALL:` bounds by `cpu->machine->ncpus`; common tail `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memory_writemax64(...); return 1;`. TRUE.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:30:- **`reassert_interrupts` has exactly four callers** (`grep -n 'reassert_interrupts(' src/devices/dev_luna88k.c`): the two device-line handlers and the two store arms. So the corollary's path (a shared line after an arming store walks the absent slot) is REAL, not inferred.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:35:**F3. THE SIO LINE-5 ROUTE IS SYNCHRONOUS AND GUEST-DRIVABLE WHILE STEPPING (kimi UPHELD, verified).** Anchor `case OBIO_SIO + 4:` (cmd channel 0, 0x51000004): first write with `obio_sio_regno == 0` selects `regnr = idata & 7` (store 0x01 -> WR1); second write lands `d->obio_sio_wr[sio_devnr][regnr] = idata`; then `if (d->obio_sio_wr[sio_devnr][SCC_WR1] & SCC_WR1_TX_IE && !old_tx_enable) d->sio_tx_pending[sio_devnr] = 1;` -> `reassert_serial_interrupt(d)` -> `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->sio_irq)` -> `luna88k_interrupt_assert` (connected to `%s.luna88k.5`, `templ.line = 5`) -> `interrupt_status |= 1 << 30` -> `reassert_interrupts(d)`. `SCC_WR1_TX_IE` = 0x02 (`src/include/thirdparty/sccreg.h`). Two word stores, no host timer. **Disarm is also synchronous**: a cmd write of 0x28 (`SCC_RESET_TX_IP`) -> `sio_tx_pending = 0` -> `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->sio_irq)` -> bit 30 cleared -> `reassert_interrupts` — which, under an ASSERT-only NULL check, is a DEASSERT of NULL: the disarm doubles as the M_NULLCHECK row. Session-order caveat: bit 30 stays pending machine-wide until disarmed, so place the arming after W1 or disarm at once, or the pre-fix session dies on an arming store instead of a SOFT store.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:37:**F4. Why F3's row is MANDATORY — the smallest edit that closes W1 and leaves a death (Q2).** Bound the reassert CALL in the two store arms only (`if (cpunr < ncpus) reassert_interrupts(d);`, loop untouched, stores still land). W1 survives (no walk after SOFT_INT1), the SOFT_INT1 read survives, `soft1x2`/`mask2`/`soft2`/`mask3`/`soft3` survive, `slot0` survives (slot 1 not yet armed when A1 runs). **Every store-arm row stays green**, and the first device line after arming — the SIO row in a cold debugger, the clock on a running guest — still walks slot 1 into NULL. Only F3's row reds. Three more smallest edits, each with its surviving-green row: `if (cpu == 1) continue;` -> slots 2/3 still die -> `mask2/soft2` HALT (this, not M_BOUND2, is what the slot-2/3 rows kill: `cpu < 2` is already killed by W1 on 1 cpu and is EQUIVALENT to (a) on `-n 2`); ASSERT-only NULL check -> dies on the DEASSERT (see F6); `d->ncpus = 1` hardcoded -> every 1-cpu row green, only the `-n 2` vector row reds.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:39:**F5. The MASK-readback delivery channel is code-refuted (glm/grok/kimi UPHELD; deepseek OVERTURNED).** Read path: `uint32_t status = d->interrupt_status & currentMask;` — the software bit is OR'd only into reassert's LOCAL `status` and never written to `d->interrupt_status`. Bits 31..29 show hardware lines only, independent of connection and of whether `reassert_interrupts` ran at all. It IS a fine channel for F3's row (after TX_IE arming with mask bit 30, `highestCurrentStatus = 5` -> readback `0xa0000000 | 0x00fc0000`) but it observes `interrupt_status`, not delivery, so it cannot kill M_STOREBUTNOREASSERT.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:70:**Q1.** No premise is wrong (F1). `interrupt_status` is one machine-wide field, software interrupts per slot; default count 1 and the rig boots with it; nothing else connects `cpu_irq[1..3]`; both arms are unbounded; four callers of `reassert_interrupts` make the corollary real, subject to F2's mask condition. Two premises the brief did not state and should: (i) W1 is lethal only because A2 armed slot 1 earlier in the SAME session (glm) — a per-row-session refactor makes `soft1` vacuously green; (ii) `step` advances every running cpu (F7).
